# Rank 1090 / CVE-2020-15133: faye-websocket-ruby's client connected wss: URLs
# with EventMachine::Connection#start_tls and never set :verify_peer, and
# EventMachine's default for that option is off, so every wss: connection this
# library made accepted any certificate for any hostname with no verification
# at all (CWE-295). lib/faye/websocket/client.rb had
#
#   if secure
#     socket_tls = { :sni_hostname => URI.parse(@url).host }.merge(@socket_tls)
#     @stream.start_tls(socket_tls)
#   end
#
# with no :verify_peer anywhere and no ssl_verify_peer/ssl_handshake_completed
# callbacks implemented on the Connection module, so EventMachine's optional
# verification hook was simply never used. Faye's own advisory frames this as
# the same underlying gap independently discovered and fixed in
# igrigorik/em-http-request (a sibling, unrelated project, not a dependency of
# this one) around the same time. The fix (merge f41e67c428, PR #129) adds
# :verify_peer => true to the merged tls_options, and implements
# ssl_verify_peer/ssl_handshake_completed via a new SslVerifier class that
# checks the certificate chain and the hostname.
#
# Corpus correction: pool.tsv named igrigorik/em-http-request for this rank,
# flagged repo!. GHSA-2v5c-755p-p4gv's own vulnerabilities field names package
# faye-websocket, and its description explicitly distinguishes this report
# from em-http-request's separate, earlier advisory for the same underlying
# EventMachine gap; the two are unrelated sibling libraries sharing one root
# cause, not a dependency relationship. Corrected in place to
# faye/faye-websocket-ruby with fix commit f41e67c428 (merge of PR #129); rank,
# CVE and language are unchanged.
#
# ATTENTION. code.CertValidationDisabled and VYQL-CFG-004 exist for CWE-295,
# but every Ruby binding for it keys on an explicit disable: verify_mode(...
# VERIFY_NONE), or a named project's presence pattern such as Kubeclient or Fog
# setting verify_ssl=false. None can state "start_tls was called with no
# verify_peer option, and the library's default for that option is off" --
# there is no call here disabling anything, only an option that was never
# turned on, and EventMachine's own default choice is what makes that
# dangerous. This is the same class of gap as rank 1053's CleartextChannel,
# read from the certificate side of the connection rather than the scheme
# side: the weakness is a library default nobody overrode, and stating it
# needs a review concept naming the option and the library, not a presence
# pattern over an explicit disable call.
#
# Scanning client.rb reports 2 findings, both unrelated library-profile
# heuristic findings (a taint-labelled write parameter and a URL fetch), and
# vyql match labels no source or sink around start_tls.
#
# verify_current_expected: attention-vocabulary-gap

test "rank1090: start_tls is called with no verify_peer option set"
  lang ruby
  file lib/faye/websocket/client.rb
  reject VYQL-CFG-004
  code
  ```
  module Faye
    module WebSocket
      class Client
        def initialize(url, protocols = nil, options = {})
          @url = url
          @origin_tls = options.fetch(:tls, {})
          @socket_tls = @origin_tls
        end

        def on_connect(stream)
          @stream = stream
          secure = @url.start_with?('wss:')

          if secure
            socket_tls = { :sni_hostname => URI.parse(@url).host }.merge(@socket_tls)
            @stream.start_tls(socket_tls)
          end
        end
      end
    end
  end
  ```

test "rank1090: the fix enables verify_peer and implements the callbacks"
  lang ruby
  file lib/faye/websocket/client.rb
  reject VYQL-CFG-004
  code
  ```
  module Faye
    module WebSocket
      class Client
        def initialize(url, protocols = nil, options = {})
          @url = url
          @origin_tls = options.fetch(:tls, {})
          @socket_tls = @origin_tls
        end

        def start_tls(uri, options)
          return unless uri.scheme == 'wss'

          tls_options = { :sni_hostname => uri.host, :verify_peer => true }.merge(options)
          @ssl_verifier = SslVerifier.new(uri.host, tls_options)
          @stream.start_tls(tls_options)
        end

        def on_connect(stream)
          @stream = stream
          start_tls(URI.parse(@url), @socket_tls)
        end

        def ssl_verify_peer(cert)
          @ssl_verifier.ssl_verify_peer(cert)
        end

        def ssl_handshake_completed
          @ssl_verifier.ssl_handshake_completed
        end
      end
    end
  end
  ```
